{%- comment -%}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Size-chart registry — chart definitions
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The store ships ONE global size chart from /pages/size-chart that every PDP
  size-guide surface (sticky size guide, sleek quick-add sheet, VTO sheet and the
  variant-picker modal) renders. This island defines product-specific charts that
  override it; which product gets which chart is resolved by
  snippets/size-chart-key.liquid (matched by handle / tag / product type).

  Each chart:
    • title / sub / note — header copy (sub & title are hidden in the sticky and
      sleek sheets, shown in the VTO and variant-picker modals).
    • unit     — the unit the values are written in ("in" or "cm"). Charts are
                 authored in whichever unit their spec sheet was written in, and
                 the Inches/Centimeters toggle derives the other. Author in the
                 unit that survives the round trip: in→cm rounds to whole
                 centimetres, so a chart whose cm figures carry a half (106.5)
                 must be authored in cm or those halves are lost. cm→in rounds
                 to the half inch, which is the precision these tables are cut
                 to, so authoring in cm is lossless in both directions.
    • display  — the unit shown first ("in" or "cm").
    • cols     — columns in display order: { key, label }.
    • rows     — one object per size; keys match the col keys plus "size".
    • fit      — one line describing how the garment is cut. Shown above the
                 table and reused in the finder's explanation.
    • solve    — which measurement "Find my size" works from: { key, metric,
                 noun }. Absent means chest, which is every top. A bottom sets
                 { "key": "waist", "metric": "waist", "noun": "waist" } so the
                 ladder reads the waist column, the body model switches to the
                 waist regression, and the copy says "waist" instead of "chest".
    • stretch  — how much an elasticated band gives beyond its relaxed
                 measurement, in the chart's own unit. Its presence is what tells the finder this is an
                 elastic waist, where the relaxed band is MEANT to sit under the
                 body. Omit on anything woven to a fixed measurement.
    • ease     — HOW MUCH ROOM the garment is cut with, in the chart's own unit:
                 garment chest minus the wearer's chest, at the intended fit.
                 This is the number "Find my size" solves against, so it encodes
                 the design intent of the category:
                   polo    +2  regular fit, meant to sit close
                   tee     +5  oversized, drop shoulder
                   shirt   +5  relaxed cuban cut
                   hoodie +16  see below
                   jogger  -5  see below (cm, like the rest of that chart)

                 The jogger's ease is NEGATIVE and that is not a typo. Every
                 chart above is woven to a fixed measurement, so the garment
                 must be bigger than the body. A jogger's waist is elastic: the
                 relaxed band is cut UNDER the body and the elastic closes the
                 gap. -5 asks for a band about five centimetres (two inches)
                 under the wearer's waist, which grips without digging and
                 leaves most of the 4-6 inches of stretch unused.

                 Note the unit: ease is read in the chart's OWN unit, and this
                 chart is authored in centimetres. Writing -2 here would ask for
                 2 cm, not 2 inches, and hand a 175 cm / 70 kg shopper an XL.
                 Get this wrong and the finder confidently recommends the wrong
                 size, so it belongs next to the measurements it describes —
                 not buried in JS.

                 The hoodie's +16 looks wrong beside the others and is not. Its
                 chest column sits a constant +11" over the t-shirt at EVERY
                 size (51/53/55… against 40/42/44…), so 5 + 11 = 16 is simply
                 the tee's ease carried across that offset. The effect is that
                 the finder returns the same letter a shopper wears in our tees
                 — which is the intent, since the two ladders grade in lockstep.
                 Using the tee's +5 here would return XS to every shopper under
                 about 110 kg, because XS is already 51" around.
    • faqs     — optional per-chart overrides; anything omitted falls back to
                 "defaults.faqs" below.

  Half-inch values (9.5, 10.5 …) are preserved — the client renders this data
  directly instead of going through the global pipeline, which rounds inches to
  whole numbers. The polo chart depends on this: 16.5 / 17.5 shoulder and 8.5
  sleeve would all be rounded away on the global page.

  "defaults" holds everything that is the same for every category: the build
  ladder the finder offers and the standing FAQ copy. Both are merchant-editable
  here rather than in assets/theme.js, because they are copy, not logic.

  Note the build offsets are in INCHES and are applied to the estimated chest.
  They are deliberately small: the estimate already carries the shopper's weight,
  so build is a nudge for how that weight is distributed, not a second guess at
  the same thing.

  To add a category: add a chart here, then add a matching rule in
  snippets/size-chart-key.liquid.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{%- endcomment -%}
